def article(path,series,num,title,summary,sections,original=None,lab=False):
 toc=''.join(f'<a href="#s{i}">{h}</a>' for i,(h,b) in enumerate(sections))
 body=''.join(f'<section id="s{i}"><h2>{h}</h2>{b}</section>' for i,(h,b) in enumerate(sections))
 source=f'<a class="textlink" href="{original}">Read the original on LinkedIn ↗</a>' if original else f'<a class="textlink" href="{NEWS}">Visit the PCIe newsletter ↗</a>'
 nxt=button('/width-vs-frequency/','Continue to Part 1.1: the lab') if lab else button('/pcie/','Back to the PCIe series',True)
 c=f'''<section class="articleintro"><a class="breadcrumb" href="/{'soc-architecture' if lab else 'pcie'}/">← {series}</a><p class="eyebrow">{series} / PART {num}</p><h1>{title}</h1><p class="lede">{summary}</p><div class="articlemeta"><span class="avatar">SG</span><span>By Srinivas Gaddam</span><span>Technical guide</span><button class="copylink">Copy link</button></div></section><div class="readinglayout"><aside class="toc"><b>IN THIS ARTICLE</b>{toc}{source}</aside><article class="prose">{body}<div class="endnote"><p>Explore the assumptions. Follow the evidence. Keep the system boundary visible.</p>{nxt}</div></article></div>'''
 write(path+'/index.html',shell(title,summary,c,'soc' if lab else 'pcie'))

socsections=[
('The engineering question','<p>A 512 bit datapath at 1 GHz must deliver twice its useful throughput. Should we widen it to 1024 bits, raise the clock to 2 GHz, or use two engines?</p><p>First check where bandwidth is lost. An engine waiting for memory needs a different solution from one moving partially filled words. Consider an internal DMA payload path between a PCIe subsystem and the SoC fabric. Both ends must support the target.</p><div class="note">The operating points are illustrative design assumptions, not vendor measurements or PCIe link rates.</div>'),
('Peak capacity is only the beginning','<div class="tablewrap"><table><tr><th>Architecture</th><th>Peak capacity</th></tr><tr><td>512 bits at 1 GHz</td><td>64 GB/s</td></tr><tr><td>1024 bits at 1 GHz</td><td>128 GB/s</td></tr><tr><td>512 bits at 2 GHz</td><td>128 GB/s</td></tr><tr><td>Two 512 bit engines at 1 GHz</td><td>128 GB/s aggregate</td></tr></table></div><p>These one direction rates assume one accepted word per cycle. If the original sustains 48 GB/s, the target is 96 GB/s. A useful payload target of 128 GB/s leaves no room for overhead in a nominal 128 GB/s design.</p><div class="formula">Useful throughput = (width ÷ 8) × frequency × transfer utilization × useful byte occupancy</div><p>For example, 128 GB/s × 0.85 × 0.90 gives 97.92 GB/s, only 2% above the target. For two engines, calculate their rates separately and add them.</p>'),
('When widening helps','<p>A wider engine suits long payloads when the processing lanes, buffers and fabric can move more data. An unchanged 512 bit port at 1 GHz still limits that path to 64 GB/s. A width converter does not create bandwidth.</p><p>Suppose each data item contains 48 bytes and the engine accepts one item per beat, without packing. At 1 GHz, both a 512 bit design and a 1024 bit design deliver 48 GB/s. Widening helps this workload only if the design can pack and process multiple items together.</p><p>Inspect size and alignment distributions. A FIFO with 64 entries stores 4 KiB at 512 bits and 8 KiB at 1024 bits, before metadata. Register load, muxes, control fanout and routing also change.</p>'),
('What changes at 2 GHz','<p>The period falls from 1 ns to 0.5 ns. Clock to Q, setup, uncertainty and skew leave less time for logic and wires. With an illustrative 100 ps allowance, the remaining budget falls from 900 ps to 400 ps.</p><p>Pipelining can shorten a logic path, but data, tags, byte enables and errors must remain aligned. Feedback may still limit how often new work enters. Memory macros, crossings and the downstream fabric must support the higher service rate.</p><p>Eight stages at 1 GHz and sixteen at 2 GHz both give 8 ns of nominal pipeline latency. Higher throughput does not necessarily mean a shorter completion time for one operation.</p>'),
('What two engines require','<p>Replication retains the local clock target but adds control state, dispatch and possibly result ordering. Independent descriptors or flows are natural candidates. A single dependency chain cannot keep both engines busy without another mechanism.</p><p>Both engines need enough aggregate input and output bandwidth. Shared SRAM ports, memory banks and merge logic can become bottlenecks. If one engine sustains 48 GB/s and two sustain 80 GB/s, scaling efficiency is 80 ÷ 96 = 83.3%. The pair still misses the target.</p><p>Inspect each engine separately. An average utilization can hide uneven work distribution or source starvation.</p>'),
('Compare energy and physical cost','<div class="formula">Dynamic power ≈ activity × capacitance × voltage² × frequency</div><p>Doubling frequency doubles this component only when everything else stays constant. If voltage rises from an assumed 0.75 V to 0.90 V, the factor becomes 2 × (0.90 ÷ 0.75)² = 2.88. That is a conditional example, not a process requirement.</p><p>Widening or replication also changes switched capacitance. Compare energy per useful byte over the same workload, including clock power, leakage, buffering and crossings. For bursty traffic, include idle time and wakeup overhead.</p><p>If wires dominate, examine locality and placement. Wider buses increase connectivity demand; faster clocks give long routes less time. Two local engines help only when their data and buffers can remain local.</p>'),
('Make a provisional architecture decision','<p>Without physical trials, choose a candidate with explicit conditions. Record the target throughput and latency, workload traces, memory service, clock assumptions, macro limits, expected placement and power accounting boundary.</p><ul><li><b>Packed payloads and matching wide interfaces:</b> investigate 1024 bits at 1 GHz.</li><li><b>Small items with credible clock and memory evidence:</b> investigate 512 bits at 2 GHz.</li><li><b>Independent queues and separate banks:</b> investigate two 512 bit engines.</li><li><b>Shared memory is saturated:</b> improve service or data reuse first.</li></ul><p>Give each unresolved assumption an owner and a validation milestone. Keep a fallback until timing, power and routing evidence support the choice.</p>'),
('Try the interactive lab','<p>The existing lab uses its original 256/512 bit comparison. The revised article above uses 512/1024 bits. The reasoning is the same, but the absolute capacities differ. The lab makes its own configuration, power and area assumptions explicit.</p>'+button('/width-vs-frequency/','Open Width vs Frequency'))]
article('soc-architecture/part-1','SoC Architecture','1','Wider datapath or higher frequency?','Three ways to increase bandwidth. One system that must supply the data, meet timing and stay within budget.',socsections,SOC,True)

p1=[('The complete journey','<p>A GPU, SSD or network adapter can be electrically present while still invisible to software. Bringing it into use crosses several boundaries: power and reset, receiver detection, link training, configuration enumeration, driver binding and data movement.</p><div class="journey">Power & reset → Detect → Train → L0 → Enumerate → Driver</div>'),('Link bring up','<p>Power and clock stability allow the ports to begin their work. Link training establishes communication across the lanes. Reaching L0 is an important physical link milestone, but it does not mean that software has discovered the Function or allocated its resources.</p>'),('Software discovery','<p>Firmware and the operating system discover Functions through configuration access. Resource allocation and driver initialization follow. When bring up fails, identify the last boundary that has actually succeeded instead of treating the whole process as one event.</p>'),('Read the original article',f'<p>This overview introduces the first published article. Continue on LinkedIn for the complete explanation and original figures.</p>{button(LINKS[1],"Read Part 1 on LinkedIn")}')]
article('pcie/part-1','PCIe Systems','1',pcie_titles[0],pcie_desc[0],p1,LINKS[1])
p2=[('Start with the symptom','<p>Power is good, reset is released and Receiver Detect succeeds. Yet the expected Gen6 x16 link trains at x8, remains at a lower speed, or repeatedly enters Recovery. Those observations point to different boundaries inside training.</p><p>The useful question is: what is the last thing the link definitely proved?</p>'),('Read the LTSSM as evidence','<p>The Link Training and Status State Machine turns an electrical connection into an operational link. Detect establishes receiver presence. Polling establishes recognizable training exchange. Configuration establishes the link’s lane arrangement. Recovery handles retraining and speed related work.</p><p>Do not infer the negotiated width or speed from capabilities alone. Compare supported capability with current link status.</p>'),('Separate width from speed','<div class="tablewrap"><table><tr><th>Observation</th><th>Investigation</th></tr><tr><td>Expected x16, negotiated x8</td><td>Detected lanes, connectivity and lane configuration</td></tr><tr><td>Expected higher speed, stable lower speed</td><td>Speed change and equalization evidence</td></tr><tr><td>Repeated Recovery</td><td>Training sequence, signal quality and error correlation</td></tr></table></div><p>Capture the sequence of states and transitions. A single snapshot can hide a repeated failure loop.</p>'),('Connect hardware and software','<p>Correlate port status with PHY observations and protocol traces. Keep link training separate from Data Link initialization and configuration enumeration. A later software discovery failure does not automatically mean the LTSSM failed.</p>'),('Continue with the full article',f'<p>The published article follows Detect, Polling, Configuration and Recovery in more detail, including the Gen6 and Gen7 perspective.</p>{button(LINKS[2],"Read Part 2 on LinkedIn")}')]
article('pcie/part-2','PCIe Systems','2',pcie_titles[1],pcie_desc[1],p2,LINKS[2])
p3=[('A healthy link is not enough','<p>Assume the link is stable and the Data Link Layer is active, but the operating system still cannot discover the endpoint. The next investigation is the configuration transaction path.</p><p>Discovery depends on reaching the Function and receiving a valid Completion. BAR allocation is a later boundary.</p>'),('Follow bus routing','<p>Software identifies a Function using Bus, Device and Function within a PCI segment. Bridge bus number registers define which bus numbers exist below each bridge. Incorrect secondary or subordinate bus programming can prevent a Configuration Request from reaching the endpoint.</p><div class="journey">Root Port → Switch bridge Functions → Endpoint</div>'),('Type 0 and Type 1 requests','<p>Configuration routing uses Type 1 requests to travel through the hierarchy toward the target bus, with Type 0 access on the target bus. These request types should not be confused with the Type 0 and Type 1 configuration header layouts.</p>'),('Debug one boundary at a time','<ol><li>Confirm stable link and Data Link status.</li><li>Record the intended topology and bus numbering.</li><li>Trace the Configuration Request through the bridge hierarchy.</li><li>Check whether a Completion returns and whether it reports success.</li><li>Correlate the hardware trace with the operating system’s discovery attempt.</li></ol><p>At Gen6 and Gen7 rates, decode the physical transport before interpreting the configuration transaction. A retimer is a physical observation point, not another enumerated bridge Function.</p>'),('The next boundary',f'<p>Once identity reads succeed, the endpoint is configuration visible. Making its registers usable requires resources and an address path. That is the subject of Part 4.</p>{button(LINKS[3],"Read Part 3 on LinkedIn")}')]
article('pcie/part-3','PCIe Systems','3',pcie_titles[2],pcie_desc[2],p3,LINKS[3])
p4=[('Visible does not mean usable','<p>The endpoint appears as 03:00.0. Configuration reads return its Vendor ID, Device ID and capabilities. The driver matches the Function, but BAR0 has no assigned address.</p><p>Configuration visibility proves that the configuration route works. MMIO usability also requires a valid address allocation, bridge windows covering that address and Memory Space Enable at the endpoint.</p>'),('BARs and bridge windows have different jobs','<p>A Base Address Register describes an endpoint resource aperture. A 32 bit memory BAR uses one register position; a 64 bit BAR uses an adjacent pair. A bridge memory window determines which address ranges the bridge forwards downstream.</p><p>Allocating the endpoint BAR is insufficient if a parent bridge cannot forward its complete range.</p>'),('Sizing and address placement','<p>Enumeration software probes the implemented BAR size under controlled conditions and allocates an appropriately sized and aligned range. A 32 bit memory BAR must lie below 4 GB. A 64 bit memory BAR can lie below or above 4 GB.</p><p>Available host bridge apertures and reserved regions still constrain allocation. A 64 bit CPU does not by itself guarantee sufficient MMIO space. Above 4G decoding is a platform firmware option related to that resource map; it does not change the endpoint’s DMA address width.</p><div class="note">Do not manually size a live BAR. Use the operating system’s resource information and controlled enumeration procedures.</div>'),('A worked allocation failure','<p>Consider a 256 MB, 64 bit prefetchable resource using the BAR0/BAR1 pair. The downstream bridge window provides only 128 MB and cannot grow in the current map. The endpoint remains configuration visible, but there is no valid allocation path for that resource.</p><p>This is an illustrative address map, not a claim about the aperture size of a particular accelerator. The repair is to provide a suitable region and valid windows throughout the hierarchy, then enable memory decoding.</p>'),('Follow the driver in order','<div class="journey">Resource assigned → Region reserved → BAR mapped → Register access</div><p>On Linux, device enable, region reservation and BAR mapping are separate steps. Check every return value. Use the PCI core’s resource records: CPU physical addresses and PCI bus addresses are not necessarily identical on every platform.</p>'),('A practical debug sequence','<ol><li>Save the topology, boot log, configuration space and resource map.</li><li>Confirm stable configuration reads for the endpoint.</li><li>Inspect BAR type, size, alignment and assigned range.</li><li>Check the host bridge’s available MMIO apertures.</li><li>Walk every parent bridge window and verify full containment.</li><li>Check endpoint Memory Space Enable.</li><li>Identify the exact driver step that fails.</li></ol><p>Gen6 and Gen7 increase transport capability, but these resource and driver boundaries still require separate validation.</p>'),('About this edition',f'<p>This web guide is adapted from the Part 4 source manuscript. The series continues from CPU initiated register access toward endpoint initiated DMA and address translation.</p>{button(NEWS,"Visit the PCIe newsletter",True)}')]
article('pcie/part-4','PCIe Systems','4',pcie_titles[3],pcie_desc[3],p4)

lab=f'''<section class="labintro"><div><a class="breadcrumb" href="/soc-architecture/part-1/">← Part 1: the architecture decision</a><p class="eyebrow">SOC ARCHITECTURE / PART 1.1</p><h1>Width vs Frequency</h1><p>Change the assumptions. Compare the architectures. Inspect the evidence.</p></div><a class="button secondary" href="/lab-app/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open full screen ↗</a></section><div class="labguide"><b>Start here</b><span>1. Set your payload target</span><span>2. Adjust workload losses</span><span>3. Inspect the decision and sensitivity</span></div><details class="labnote"><summary>Model scope and article configuration</summary><p>This is the existing lab with its original presets: 512 bits at 1 GHz, 256 bits at 2 GHz, and two 256 bit engines at 1 GHz. Each has 64 GB/s raw capacity. The revised article uses 512/1024 bits and a 128 GB/s comparison. Power and area are editable scale estimates, not measurements of these designs. Read Model & evidence inside the lab before using results. Changes stay in this browser session.</p></details><iframe class="labframe" title="Width vs Frequency interactive architecture lab" src="/lab-app/"></iframe>'''
